<DocsLayout
  title="Getting Started"
  description="Structure-D in 5 minutes — from installation to your first extraction."
  toc={toc}
  editPath="/docs/getting-started.astro"
  next={{ label: 'Installation', href: `${base}docs/installation` }}
>
  <h2 id="what-is-structure-d">What is Structure-D?</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Structure-D</strong> is a modular pipeline framework that converts unstructured documents
    (PDFs, images, HTML, DOCX, and more) into <strong>validated, schema-constrained structured data</strong> using LLMs.
  </p>
  <p>It is designed around three principles:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Format-focused, not domain-specific</strong> — Structure-D handles file formats, not document types. You define what you want to extract.</li>
    <li><strong>Schema-driven</strong> — Your Pydantic model is the contract. Structure-D guarantees the output matches it.</li>
    <li><strong>Async-first</strong> — Every I/O operation is <code>async</code>. Process thousands of documents in parallel.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout info">
    <span class="callout-icon">i</span>
    <div class="callout-body">
      <p class="callout-title">Currently in beta</p>
      <p class="callout-content">Structure-D v0.2.0 is production-ready for Phase 1 features. ETL scheduling and HITL workflows are on the roadmap for v0.3.0.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>
  <p>Every document flows through a six-stage pipeline:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Ingestion</strong> — a format-appropriate parser extracts raw text, tables, and images from your file</li>
    <li><strong>Preprocessing</strong> — text is normalized, cleaned, and chunked into optimal-size segments</li>
    <li><strong>Model Routing</strong> — the router selects the best registered model for your task and schema</li>
    <li><strong>Inference</strong> — the LLM provider generates structured output constrained to your schema via <code>guided_json</code></li>
    <li><strong>Validation</strong> — every output is validated against the Pydantic model; invalid outputs trigger an automatic retry with a refined prompt</li>
    <li><strong>Storage</strong> — results are written to JSONL, CSV, or a database destination</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="pipeline-diagram">
    <div class="pd-step">
      <span class="pd-abbr" style="color:#2563eb">IN</span>
      <span class="pd-label">Ingest</span>
    </div>
    <span class="pd-arrow">&rarr;</span>
    <div class="pd-step">
      <span class="pd-abbr" style="color:#7c3aed">PP</span>
      <span class="pd-label">Preprocess</span>
    </div>
    <span class="pd-arrow">&rarr;</span>
    <div class="pd-step">
      <span class="pd-abbr" style="color:#d97706">RT</span>
      <span class="pd-label">Route</span>
    </div>
    <span class="pd-arrow">&rarr;</span>
    <div class="pd-step">
      <span class="pd-abbr" style="color:#16a34a">LLM</span>
      <span class="pd-label">Infer</span>
    </div>
    <span class="pd-arrow">&rarr;</span>
    <div class="pd-step">
      <span class="pd-abbr" style="color:#dc2626">VL</span>
      <span class="pd-label">Validate</span>
    </div>
    <span class="pd-arrow">&rarr;</span>
    <div class="pd-step">
      <span class="pd-abbr" style="color:#0891b2">ST</span>
      <span class="pd-label">Store</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="quick-start">Quick start</h2>
  <p>Install Structure-D with the extras you need:</p>

  <div class="code-block-wrapper">
    <div class="code-block-header">
      <span class="code-block-lang">bash</span>
      <button class="code-block-copy">Copy</button>
    </div>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">pip install "structure-d[ingestion,api,llm]"</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p>Then extract structured data from any document:</p>

  <div class="code-block-wrapper">
    <div class="code-block-header">
      <span class="code-block-filename">quickstart.py</span>
      <span class="code-block-lang">python</span>
      <button class="code-block-copy">Copy</button>
    </div>
    <pre><code class="language-python">import asyncio
from pathlib import Path
from pydantic import BaseModel
from structure_d.pipeline import Pipeline
from structure_d.inference.providers import OpenAIProvider

# 1. Define your extraction schema as a Pydantic model
class Invoice(BaseModel):
    vendor: str
    invoice_number: str
    total_amount: float
    line_items: list[str]
    due_date: str | None = None

# 2. Create the pipeline
async def main():
    pipeline = Pipeline(
        schema_cls=Invoice,
        provider=OpenAIProvider(),   # or AnthropicProvider(), OllamaProvider(), VLLMProvider()
    )

    # 3. Run on a file — format is auto-detected
    results = await pipeline.run(Path("invoice.pdf"))

    for result in results:
        print(result.structured_output)
        # &#123;"vendor": "Acme Corp", "invoice_number": "INV-001", "total_amount": 1240.00, ...&#125;

asyncio.run(main())</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p>
    Structure-D automatically detects the file format, selects the appropriate parser,
    chunks the content, runs inference, and validates the output — retrying up to 3 times
    if the model produces invalid JSON.
  </p>

  <div class="callout tip">
    <span class="callout-icon"><svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M8 1.5a4.5 4.5 0 1 0 0 9 4.5 4.5 0 0 0 0-9zM0 6a8 8 0 1 1 16 0A8 8 0 0 1 0 6zm8 4a.5.5 0 0 1 .5.5v1.5a.5.5 0 0 1-1 0V10.5A.5.5 0 0 1 8 10zm0-9.5a.5.5 0 0 1 .5.5V3a.5.5 0 0 1-1 0V1a.5.5 0 0 1 .5-.5z"/></svg></span>
    <div class="callout-body">
      <p class="callout-title">Use built-in schemas</p>
      <p class="callout-content">Don't want to define a schema? Use one of the built-ins: <code>key_value</code>, <code>table</code>, <code>entity</code>, <code>form</code>, <code>classification</code>, or <code>summary</code>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="code-block-wrapper">
    <div class="code-block-header">
      <span class="code-block-lang">python</span>
      <button class="code-block-copy">Copy</button>
    </div>
    <pre><code class="language-python">from structure_d.schemas.generic import get_schema

# Use a built-in schema by name
KeyValue = get_schema("key_value")
pipeline = Pipeline(schema_cls=KeyValue, provider=OpenAIProvider())</code></pre>
  </div>

  <h2 id="use-cases">Common use cases</h2>
  <p>Structure-D is built for any task where you need to pull structured fields out of a document.</p>

  <div class="use-cases-grid">
    <div class="use-case-card">
      <h4 class="uc-title">Invoice &amp; receipt processing</h4>
      <p class="uc-desc">Extract vendor name, line items, totals, dates, and tax information from PDF or scanned invoices.</p>
      <span class="uc-formats">PDF &middot; PNG &middot; JPEG</span>
    </div>
    <div class="use-case-card">
      <h4 class="uc-title">Contract analysis</h4>
      <p class="uc-desc">Pull clauses, parties, dates, obligations, and termination conditions from legal documents.</p>
      <span class="uc-formats">PDF &middot; DOCX</span>
    </div>
    <div class="use-case-card">
      <h4 class="uc-title">Form digitisation</h4>
      <p class="uc-desc">Convert paper forms, questionnaires, and survey responses into structured JSON records.</p>
      <span class="uc-formats">PDF &middot; PNG &middot; JPEG &middot; HTML</span>
    </div>
    <div class="use-case-card">
      <h4 class="uc-title">Report extraction</h4>
      <p class="uc-desc">Parse financial reports, research papers, and annual filings into tables, summaries, and key metrics.</p>
      <span class="uc-formats">PDF &middot; DOCX &middot; XLSX</span>
    </div>
    <div class="use-case-card">
      <h4 class="uc-title">Email triage</h4>
      <p class="uc-desc">Classify incoming emails, extract intent, priority, named entities, and structured action items.</p>
      <span class="uc-formats">EML &middot; MSG &middot; HTML</span>
    </div>
    <div class="use-case-card">
      <h4 class="uc-title">Knowledge base RAG</h4>
      <p class="uc-desc">Build a retrieval-augmented QA system over a document corpus with built-in vector indexing.</p>
      <span class="uc-formats">PDF &middot; DOCX &middot; HTML &middot; TXT</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="core-concepts">Core concepts</h2>

  <h3>Schema-driven extraction</h3>
  <p>
    Every extraction job is backed by a Pydantic model. Structure-D converts your model to a JSON Schema
    and passes it to the LLM as a <code>guided_json</code> constraint (vLLM) or a tool-use parameter
    (OpenAI, Anthropic). The model is forced to produce output that matches your type signature.
  </p>

  <div class="code-block-wrapper">
    <div class="code-block-header">
      <span class="code-block-lang">python</span>
      <button class="code-block-copy">Copy</button>
    </div>
    <pre><code class="language-python">from pydantic import BaseModel, Field

class Contract(BaseModel):
    parties: list[str] = Field(description="Names of all signing parties")
    effective_date: str = Field(description="Contract start date in YYYY-MM-DD format")
    termination_clause: str | None = Field(None, description="Termination conditions verbatim")
    jurisdiction: str = Field(description="Governing law / jurisdiction")</code></pre>
  </div>

  <h3>Automatic format detection</h3>
  <p>
    When you pass a <code>Path</code> to <code>pipeline.run()</code>, Structure-D inspects the file
    extension and MIME type to select the right parser automatically. You can also specify a parser explicitly.
  </p>

  <div class="prose-table-wrapper">
    <table class="prose-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Extension</th>
          <th>Default parser</th>
          <th>What gets extracted</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>.pdf</code></td><td><code>pymupdf</code></td><td>Text, tables, images (per page)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>.pdf</code> (scanned)</td><td><code>ocr_pdf</code></td><td>OCR text via Tesseract</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>.png</code>, <code>.jpg</code></td><td><code>tesseract_image</code></td><td>OCR text from images</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>.html</code>, <code>.htm</code></td><td><code>html</code></td><td>Main content, tables, links</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>.docx</code></td><td><code>docx</code></td><td>Paragraphs, tables, styles</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>.xlsx</code></td><td><code>xlsx</code></td><td>Sheet data, cell values</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>.pptx</code></td><td><code>pptx</code></td><td>Slide text, notes</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>.eml</code>, <code>.msg</code></td><td><code>email</code></td><td>Subject, body, headers, attachments</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>.txt</code>, <code>.md</code></td><td><code>plaintext</code></td><td>Raw text</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>.csv</code></td><td><code>plaintext</code></td><td>Raw text (structured CSV rows)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Validation and automatic retry</h3>
  <p>
    After inference, <code>SchemaValidator</code> runs the raw LLM output through Pydantic. If validation
    fails, <code>RetryHandler</code> sends the model a new prompt that includes the original output,
    the Pydantic error messages, and a reminder of the schema. The default retry budget is 3 attempts.
  </p>

